INTERPRETATION

The accepted bounded learned-U authority has now passed a stronger
developmental perturbation in the transferred compositional Task-2
environment.

The unavailable-cell footprint was doubled from 8 distributed cells
to 16 distributed cells, or from 12.5 percent to 25 percent of the ring,
for the same 12-epoch lesion window.

No other challenge axis changed.

Task load remained one request per epoch.

Expiry remained 32 epochs.

Task computation, corruption, repair, verification, and relocation semantics
were unchanged.

Learned authority remained fixed at 25 percent of U recruitment only.

The learned model still had no lesion indicator or global task information.

Despite 69 realized learned-controlled recruitment differences, every paired
world preserved throughput, backlog, cost, streams, recovery, correctness,
repair, and matching integrity.

Therefore the transferred learned recruitment behavior is not only
Task-2-capable; it remains functionally contained under a substantially
larger distributed local damage event.

BOUNDARY

Do not increase alpha automatically.

Do not expand learned authority to C/S.

Do not add online adaptation or self-modification.

NEXT SCIENTIFIC QUESTION

Change perturbation structure while holding perturbation size, duration,
Task-2 load, and learned authority fixed.

The smallest discriminating next test is a 16-cell spatially contiguous
lesion for the same 12 epochs.

This asks whether robustness to distributed 25-percent damage transfers to
locally concentrated damage, where neighborhood connectivity and local
service coverage are challenged differently.

PLAIN-SPEAK INTERPRETATION

This one worked too.

We knocked out twice as many distributed cells as before, but gave the
neural recruiter no more power and no hint that damage had happened.

It still made real developmental decisions that differed from the
hand-written recruiter.

And across all ten fair worlds, the organism kept pace with the exact parent.

No wrong answers.

No expired jobs.

No repair failures.

No recovery slowdown outside the frozen bounds.

So the learned recruitment behavior has now survived both a different
two-step task and a much larger distributed injury.

The next useful question is not more authority.

It is whether the same behavior survives when the same sixteen-cell injury
is concentrated into one local region instead of spread around the ring.

